1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,975
Whampoa: The Merchant Who
Brought Singapore Its First Ice

2
00:00:03,975 --> 00:00:06,957
Most Singaporeans know
Whampoa as a place —

3
00:00:07,037 --> 00:00:11,586
a housing estate near Novena and
Balestier, a hawker centre whose

4
00:00:11,666 --> 00:00:15,725
sign spells the name out in
giant letters — not a person.

5
00:00:15,725 --> 00:00:21,110
Almost nobody connects it to Hoo Ah Kay,
the 19th-century merchant behind it,

6
00:00:21,190 --> 00:00:24,375
who at various points
supplied the Royal Navy,

7
00:00:24,455 --> 00:00:29,486
imported Singapore's first ice, sat
on the colonial Legislative Council,

8
00:00:29,566 --> 00:00:34,250
and served as honorary consul for
three different empires at once.

9
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:38,086
I never lived in Whampoa myself,
but I've known the name my

10
00:00:38,166 --> 00:00:41,950
whole life — it's right next
to Hougang, where I grew up.

11
00:00:41,950 --> 00:00:45,550
I never once wondered who
it actually belonged to,

12
00:00:45,630 --> 00:00:47,175
until I went looking.

13
00:00:47,175 --> 00:00:49,994
Hoo Ah Kay was born in 1816 in Whampoa,

14
00:00:50,074 --> 00:00:54,008
a district of Canton — today's
Huangpu, in Guangzhou —

15
00:00:54,088 --> 00:00:58,250
which gave him the name
Singapore would remember him by.

16
00:00:58,250 --> 00:01:02,463
He arrived in Singapore in 1830,
still a boy, to work alongside

17
00:01:02,543 --> 00:01:05,530
his father at the family's
provisioning firm,

18
00:01:05,610 --> 00:01:10,028
Whampoa & Co. He inherited the
business on his father's death and,

19
00:01:10,108 --> 00:01:14,662
by 1840, had built it into a
shipchandler to the British Royal Navy,

20
00:01:14,742 --> 00:01:18,150
supplying the frigates
that called at the harbour.

21
00:01:18,150 --> 00:01:20,330
That role grew into a genuine

22
00:01:20,410 --> 00:01:23,450
friendship with one particular officer.

23
00:01:23,450 --> 00:01:27,982
Henry Keppel, the aristocratic
son of the 4th Earl of Albemarle,

24
00:01:28,062 --> 00:01:32,162
first sailed into Singapore
as a young officer in 1832 and

25
00:01:32,242 --> 00:01:35,333
kept coming back for
the next seven decades,

26
00:01:35,413 --> 00:01:41,099
eventually helping suppress piracy across
the Malay Archipelago in the 1840s and

27
00:01:41,179 --> 00:01:45,575
surveying the deep-water anchorage
then known as New Harbour.

28
00:01:45,575 --> 00:01:50,011
He revisited Singapore once
more in 1900, at the age of 92;

29
00:01:50,091 --> 00:01:55,445
the colonial government renamed the
harbour Keppel Harbour on the spot,

30
00:01:55,525 --> 00:01:59,275
in his honour, four years
before his death at 95.

31
00:01:59,275 --> 00:02:03,705
Whampoa was his regular provisioner
across that entire span —

32
00:02:03,785 --> 00:02:07,925
a business relationship that
outlasted most friendships.

33
00:02:07,925 --> 00:02:11,829
In 1854 Whampoa partnered
with a fellow merchant,

34
00:02:11,909 --> 00:02:16,950
Gilbert Angus, on something
genuinely new to the tropics: ice.

35
00:02:16,950 --> 00:02:20,173
Blocks were cut from frozen
lakes in the United States,

36
00:02:20,253 --> 00:02:24,797
packed in thick sawdust using a technique
the American ice merchant Frederick

37
00:02:24,877 --> 00:02:28,280
Tudor had pioneered to slow
the melt, and shipped across a

38
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:32,424
months-long ocean voyage to an ice
house Whampoa built at Boat Quay —

39
00:02:32,504 --> 00:02:36,268
on land the colonial government
gave him in exchange for his own

40
00:02:36,348 --> 00:02:40,351
60-acre nutmeg plantation at Tanglin,
a stretch of ground that would

41
00:02:40,431 --> 00:02:44,275
later become the founding site
of the Singapore Botanic Gardens.

42
00:02:44,275 --> 00:02:48,200
It was a bet on demand
that didn't exist yet.

43
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:51,650
The partners projected sales of 1,000

44
00:02:51,730 --> 00:02:55,450
pounds of ice a day and got 400 to 500.

45
00:02:55,450 --> 00:02:59,349
Within three years, on
the 30th of November 1857,

46
00:02:59,429 --> 00:03:05,600
Whampoa withdrew from the partnership,
forced out by heavy financial losses.

47
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:10,075
Singapore's first taste of
ice was, commercially, a flop.

48
00:03:10,075 --> 00:03:12,900
The site outlived his failure.

49
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:17,807
An American firm, Tudor Ice, took
over in 1861 and did no better,

50
00:03:17,887 --> 00:03:20,646
but by 1865 a locally-run competitor,

51
00:03:20,726 --> 00:03:25,556
Singapore Ice Works, had taken
the building — making ice instead

52
00:03:25,636 --> 00:03:29,622
of importing it, at a third
less cost — and turned it

53
00:03:29,702 --> 00:03:34,075
into the colony's dominant
ice factory through the 1880s.

54
00:03:34,075 --> 00:03:38,851
The Victorian ice house stood at
Boat Quay for well over a century

55
00:03:38,931 --> 00:03:43,781
before it was finally torn down in
1981 to widen River Valley Road;

56
00:03:43,861 --> 00:03:48,350
a replica now occupies roughly
the same stretch of riverbank.

57
00:03:48,350 --> 00:03:52,537
Whampoa & Co. itself didn't
last much beyond his own sons —

58
00:03:52,617 --> 00:03:56,009
but the ice trade he
started at that one spot on

59
00:03:56,089 --> 00:03:59,850
the river did, for decades,
just not under his name.

60
00:03:59,850 --> 00:04:03,825
Whampoa's public career
outgrew his balance sheet.

61
00:04:03,825 --> 00:04:08,171
In 1869 he became the first
Asian nominated to the Straits

62
00:04:08,251 --> 00:04:14,046
Settlements Legislative Council; the
following year, on the 23rd of May 1870,

63
00:04:14,126 --> 00:04:18,090
he took the rarer position
of extraordinary member of

64
00:04:18,170 --> 00:04:22,825
the Executive Council — the only
Chinese man ever to hold it.

65
00:04:22,825 --> 00:04:26,721
Over the 1870s he served as
honorary consul for China,

66
00:04:26,801 --> 00:04:31,727
Japan and Russia, an unusual
concentration of diplomatic trust for a

67
00:04:31,807 --> 00:04:36,513
merchant of any background in
colonial Singapore, and on the 10th

68
00:04:36,593 --> 00:04:40,857
of May 1876 became the first
Chinese recipient of the Order

69
00:04:40,937 --> 00:04:45,575
of Saint Michael and Saint George,
appointed a Companion (CMG).

70
00:04:45,575 --> 00:04:48,985
None of it came from a title
track or a birthright —

71
00:04:49,065 --> 00:04:53,481
it came from four decades of being
useful to everyone who mattered,

72
00:04:53,561 --> 00:04:56,850
from a British admiral to
three separate empires.

73
00:04:56,850 --> 00:05:01,585
Whampoa's other legacy was more
private: a sprawling garden estate

74
00:05:01,665 --> 00:05:06,545
on Serangoon Road, decades in the
making, with an orange plantation,

75
00:05:06,625 --> 00:05:11,075
rockeries, artificial ponds, a
small menagerie and an aviary.

76
00:05:11,075 --> 00:05:14,638
It became a genuine gathering
place for Singapore's

77
00:05:14,718 --> 00:05:18,352
Chinese community and drew
a stream of distinguished

78
00:05:18,432 --> 00:05:22,575
visitors over the years,
including Admiral Keppel himself.

79
00:05:22,575 --> 00:05:26,449
After Whampoa's death in
1880 the estate passed to the

80
00:05:26,529 --> 00:05:30,110
merchant Seah Liang Seah,
who renamed it Bendemeer

81
00:05:30,190 --> 00:05:34,284
House at the governor's
suggestion and kept the tradition

82
00:05:34,364 --> 00:05:38,025
of lavish entertaining
going into the early 1900s.

83
00:05:38,025 --> 00:05:42,059
By the 1950s the mansion
had fallen into disrepair;

84
00:05:42,139 --> 00:05:48,350
the government acquired the site in 1964
and demolished it for redevelopment.

85
00:05:48,350 --> 00:05:52,397
Nothing of the garden survives
today — only the road names,

86
00:05:52,477 --> 00:05:55,975
Bendemeer and Whampoa,
sitting a short walk apart.

87
00:05:55,975 --> 00:05:59,520
Why it matters today: Hoo
Ah Kay never got a founder's

88
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:03,079
statue or a national holiday
— just a housing estate,

89
00:06:03,159 --> 00:06:06,570
a hawker centre and a stretch
of road that all carry

90
00:06:06,650 --> 00:06:10,275
a name most people walk past
without a second thought.

91
00:06:10,275 --> 00:06:13,737
His actual businesses
mostly didn't survive him;

92
00:06:13,817 --> 00:06:17,875
his name did, detached from
everything he actually did.

93
00:06:17,875 --> 00:06:21,876
It's a smaller, quieter version
of the same pattern this blog

94
00:06:21,956 --> 00:06:26,358
keeps finding in Singapore's street
map: the name outlives the man,

95
00:06:26,438 --> 00:06:29,850
and finding him again takes
actually going looking.
